{"packet_id":"42c964914b23b858","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (decide whether to invest, on mid-1962 evidence) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including dealer deepening, no expansion and a different customer group, and notes the independent dealer model as a constraint. It does not correct a false premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. It also adds an unsourced budget (EUR 20,000, 12 weeks) that later licenses an anachronistic 'online panel' test (A16) without any question about 1962 feasibility."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10","A19"],"reason":"T1 (A9) explains who changes behaviour (small-garden homeowners) and why (low cost, practicality, low maintenance). The value-capture step is unsupported: 'Deere sells tractors directly to consumers... bypassing dealers'. No source supports a direct channel. Dealer distribution is the only channel evidence (deere-wiki-dealers, cited at A14), and A1 itself flags the dealer model. The full chain to captured value is therefore not credibly traced. T2's mechanism (A10) asserts 'ownership of a lawn tractor became a visible marker of modernity and success' as fact, with no source."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (utility and convenience, broad homeowners, low price) and T2 (status, affluent buyers, premium price) are genuine rival explanations of adoption. They imply somewhat different customers and price points. Both assume the same new direct-to-consumer channel and the same product, so channel and product rivals (for example dealer-sold versus direct) are not explored."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: purchase intent direct versus via dealers, regional concentration, and premium versus standard intent among affluent households. However, T1's first assumption is 'Consumer survey data from 1962 shows interest', which presupposes data that does not exist in the dossier. The regional predictions would not clearly put either theory in trouble if they failed. T1's own prediction that direct sales beat dealer sales is never tested."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"Source classes are misread throughout. deere-ctf-competitive is a company_claim but is labelled independent_study (A12). deere-wheelhorse-demand, deere-green-76 and deere-wiki-dealers are verified_observations but are labelled independent_study (A11, A15, A14). A15 treats 'Lawn and garden equipment sales rise 76 percent' as support for T1 without noting that it is undated, possibly post-1962 (hindsight), and does not say whose sales or which market. A11 does not note that the Wheel Horse demand excerpt is undated and concerns another maker's market. The 'does not show' limits address the theories, not date, place or fit to the customer group. Evidence is cited only where it agrees."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs a single disconfirming search for T1 ('low demand') and lists the sources returned, but never reports whether any of them contradict T1. No search against T2 or against the direct-channel premise is attempted. This is a nominal check."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16","A19"],"reason":"A16 varies only the ad copy. Price is fixed at 'comparable to existing garden tools' in both arms, so the status arm does not test T2's premium-price prediction (A10). The test does not address the direct-versus-dealer value-capture premise or total demand. Yet A19 claims 'the test will reveal whether demand is sufficient'. An 'online panel' of US households is infeasible in 1962. The decision rule maps any result other than a 15-point convenience win to 'no expansion', which confuses relative message preference with absolute demand. A19's reversal conditions also contradict A16: A19 says that if 'the low-cost version outperforms, we would recommend no expansion', the opposite of A16's proceed rule. Cost, sample size and thresholds are assumptions."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"The scheduled simulated update SYN-D was delivered. A18 correctly identifies it as simulated and irrelevant (label printer, packing errors, no buyers measured) and justifiably retains T1. This is a proportionate response. It does not explicitly trace the lack of implications for the recommendation."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"A19 commits to 'Invest' and to a direct-to-consumer pilot, while also saying the test will decide whether to proceed. That is internally inconsistent. The direct channel bypassing dealers and pricing 'comparable to existing garden tools' are unsupported by any source. The reversal conditions contradict the A16 decision rule. Uncertainty about market size is stated honestly, but the position does not follow coherently from the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A17","A19"],"reason":"Value capture through direct-to-consumer sales that bypass dealers is asserted without any source support. The only channel evidence concerns independent dealers."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"The hypothesis 'ownership of a lawn tractor became a visible marker of modernity and success' is stated as fact with no source."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"Company claims and verified observations are relabelled as independent studies. deere-ctf-competitive is a company_claim presented as an independent_study."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"The undated 76% sales rise, plausibly post-1962, is used as support without date limits. This is a hindsight risk that conflicts with the A1 constraint of sources before mid-1962."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A16","A19"],"reason":"The messaging test holds price at a low level in both arms, so it cannot test T2's premium prediction. It does not test the direct-versus-dealer premise or absolute demand, although A19 claims it will reveal demand sufficiency. It is also infeasible as an online panel in 1962."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A16","A19"],"reason":"The decision rules are internally contradictory. A16 proceeds if the low-cost version wins by at least 15 points, while A19 says a low-cost win leads to no expansion."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete, but it contains material unsupported inferences: an unsourced direct-to-consumer value capture, a status claim stated as fact, and misclassified source classes. It uses possibly post-period evidence without date limits. The proposed test cannot separate the key rivals or measure demand and is anachronistic. The recommendation contradicts its own decision rule. Only the handling of the simulated update is sound."}
